From e4cb50470c3d30839470616b4824a04b6d50b43a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Kayori <thatzokay@proton.me>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 12:56:10 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] grate: put the blend stage in the last epoch, fill epochs
 with 64 packets

A fragment program of more than one epoch could not be blended:
create_blend_blob() handled one epoch only and refused the program
("72 ALU packets + 1 blend packets > 64"), so avio-qt's rounded album
picture lost its blend. NVIDIA's compiler (libcgdrv,
InsertBlendingOperation) appends the blend as the last step of the
LAST epoch and leaves the other epochs alone, measured on a 160-op
shader: 2 epochs, epoch 0 of 64 steps and 30 packets untouched, the
blend at step 51 of epoch 1.

- create_blend_epochs() rewrites the block of the last epoch with
  create_blend_blob(), checks the per-epoch limits there, and gives the
  blend variant its own copy of the epochs (variant->epochs).
  grate_fragment_blend_variant() replaces grate_fragment_blend_blob(),
  emit_epochs() takes the epochs to emit. grate_fp_epoch has the packet
  count of the epoch now.
- create_blend_blob() logs one line per limit it hits instead of a
  single failure.
- An epoch holds up to 64 ALU packets (it was 61): NVIDIA fills epochs
  with exactly 64, measured with generated shaders of increasing
  length. A program with more ALU or MFU instructions than one epoch
  holds is split into epochs even with few constants.
- GRATE_PACKING_DEBUG prints the slot operations, plain copies and
  packets per program and the ceil(ops / 4) floor; read-only, it is how
  the copy removal of the previous patch was measured.

Checked on the tablet: tools/gpu/grate-blend-epoch-test (26 non-linear
steps = 80 packets, 3 epochs) went from 2 pass / 5 fail to 7/7,
grate-blend-test is 9/9, run-grate-tests.sh has the same lines as
before this change, and avio-qt's rounded album picture looks right
with the build installed.
---
